+  (`--tests-optnone`), as #681's pragma has the checker's: a function whose
+  file's name has `_test_`, `_test.`, `_tests.`, `test_` or `fuzz` in it. No
+  source changes, and no optimizer time on the tests; their commands run a few
+  percent slower.
 - **The same input gives the same parts**, whatever the jobs: a rebuilt
   executable is the one a clean build of the same sources makes.
 
+The merge clears the parts' allocated `SHF_MERGE` flags first: a relocatable
+link merges those sections, which the final link at `-O0` copies whole from a
+single object (#2058's checker parts lost 27,904 bytes of duplicate constants
+that way). Each part's commands start once its own split is done: with CMake
+3.27 and later they take `DEPENDS_EXPLICIT_ONLY`, without which CMake makes a
+command the link uses wait for every unit's object.
+
+**What an edit repeats.** A part's job reruns only when its bitcode changed.
+In Develop, and in Release without debug information, an appended function
+changes one part and an edit that moves a file's lines that file's parts. With
+Release's `-g`, DWARF 5 records every source file's MD5 in each part that
+mentions the file, so an edit of a file repeats every part that does (most of
+the checker's for `c_check_input.c`). A comment-only edit after a file's last
+function generates nothing.
+
+**When a unit compiles whole.** `BUSTER_PARALLEL_CODEGEN` is `auto` by default:
+the build uses the parts wherever its flags allow and the tools are there, and
+otherwise says why in the configure step's `parallel native generation` line
+and compiles the units whole, in Release with the ordinary O3 pipeline. `1`
+requires the parts, failing the configuration without them; `0` never uses
+them. Sanitizers, fuzzing, PGO, an explicit host runtime and lexer variants
+keep the single compile; Debug always compiles whole. `BUSTER_BUILD_DEFINES`
+doesn't: a build at another of the checker's offsets (`BUSTER_CHECKER_OFFSET`,
+[profiling](../profiling.md)) is the product built so. The tools, beside the
+configured Clang or on `PATH`, at its version: `llvm-config` with LLVM's C API
+headers and library (the splitter is built in the tree, at
+`codegen-tools/presplit`, with the flags it gives), `clang++`, `llvm-nm`,
+`llvm-objcopy`, `llvm-readobj` and `ld.lld`. LLVM's libraries link shared
+where `llvm-config` finds them, else static, as the official release's
+archives do in CI, hosted and on the 9700X. A system library `llvm-config`
+names by a path the host lacks links by its name instead: the release names
+Ubuntu's `/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libzstd.a`, and the 9700X, an Arch host,
+links `-lzstd`.
+
+Failed splitting, a part's failed job or symbol restoration stops the graph
+before a replacement executable can be published. Diagnostics produced only
+when compiling inline assembly remain fatal under `-Werror`, but report the
+inline assembly without the original C file and line.
+
+`build-regression --parallel-codegen-only` exercises the production helpers on
+two translation units with identically named private functions, data and TLS,
+distinct hidden public entries, and shared strong and weak external references,
+in the Release configuration without debug information. It checks runtime
+isolation across threads, the source compilation database, unchanged and
+source-only edits, one-part private data edits, missing pieces, deterministic
+regeneration, late assembly warnings and failed-stage recovery. It also checks
+the mapping of `llvm-config`'s system libraries (`cmake -P`): hosted runners
+have the release's paths, so only the 9700X's build maps one. The full build
+regression also builds the real compiler in parts once and runs its baking
+checks.
